-sc-7qtuee-1 dVwCFv" role=3D"none"><h2 id=3D"why-monolithic-backend" data-i=
d=3D"f4bd02b216879ec9cf2c4ae798ff0b19">Why monolithic backend? <a class=3D"=
markdownIt-Anchor" href=3D"https://www.educative.io/courses/microservice-ar=
chitecture-practical-implementation/mE6rYo4kv30#why-monolithic-backend"><sp=
an class=3D"anchor-link">#</span></a></h2>
<p data-id=3D"979ed575fe67a40544271ed193b7743c">This application uses a mon=
olithic backend and frontend microservices because the <strong>microservice=
s</strong> lack logic and they <strong>are not self-contained systems</stro=
ng>.</p>
<p data-id=3D"81a9291c0e639c840326511261d8dc0e">However, this architecture =
can still make sense. The frontend, at least, consists of
microservices, and so <strong>independent development of the microservices =
is possible</strong>.</p>
<p data-id=3D"1a64ea18d016ad7089e3103336211ca8">Also, it is possible to <st=
rong>use different technologies in each frontend microservice</strong>. Wit=
h a large number of available UI frameworks and the high speed of innovatio=
n, that is a clear advantage.</p>
<p data-id=3D"b726c01a2ef1b5a0073ed49fb26fe5f5">Also, it is probably not po=
ssible to migrate the backend into microservices. Another team might be res=
ponsible for it. Therefore, if the scope of the project is just to improve =
the frontend, there is no
way to change the architecture of the backend.</p>
<p data-id=3D"e1049b45607aeea1d375372f17c18cb5">While SCSs are generally a =
great idea, this example shows one exception to the rule.</p>
</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class=3D"styles_=
_ViewerComponentViewStyled-sc-1xosrua-0 cvzEyH"><div><div><div><div><div cl=
ass=3D""><div class=3D""><div class=3D"markdown-container-div Markdown__Mar=
kdownContainerDiv-sc-7qtuee-5 fnZvSb" height=3D"auto"><div class=3D"markdow=
nViewer Markdown__Viewer-sc-7qtuee-1 dVwCFv" role=3D"none"><h2 id=3D"integr=
ation-with-redirects" data-id=3D"8a7209defdaba7b7cfdbf192f392d633">Integrat=
ion with redirects <a class=3D"markdownIt-Anchor" href=3D"https://www.educa=
tive.io/courses/microservice-architecture-practical-implementation/mE6rYo4k=
v30#integration-with-redirects"><span class=3D"anchor-link">#</span></a></h=
2>
<p data-id=3D"9ba5e90b774681878a51e5c1f052952f">If a user in the <em>damage=
</em> application enters a claim for a car, the
user is sent back to the overview of the car displayed on the portal.
The transition from the <em>damage</em> application to the portal is
implemented with a redirect. The <em>damage</em> application sends an HTTP
redirect after reporting the claim, leading to the web page of the
<em>main</em> application.</p>
<p data-id=3D"ce68fbb475df317ad2c2694a5d8f6539">A redirect is a very simple=
 integration. The
<em>damage</em> application needs to know only the URL for the redirect. Th=
e
portal could even pass this URL to the <em>damage</em> application to furth=
er
decouple the two applications.</p>
<p data-id=3D"caff45e116897e50edb0c13a6d3d9770">Such an integration is also=
 used if a user registers with their Google account on a web page. After th=
e user agrees to register on the Google web page, the Google page sends a r=
edirect back to the initial web page.</p>
