e=3D"none"><h2 id=3D"integration-with-links" data-id=3D"0dfe28bf41b76a8d9a1=
84cb3ccd5bd2f">Integration with links <a class=3D"markdownIt-Anchor" href=
=3D"https://www.educative.io/courses/microservice-architecture-practical-im=
plementation/mE6rYo4kv30#integration-with-links"><span class=3D"anchor-link=
">#</span></a></h2>
<p data-id=3D"779203535280be1af545cb712c1c7591">The integration of the appl=
ications is mainly done via links such as
<a href=3D"https://crimson-letter.herokuapp.com/template?contractId=3D99631=
5077&amp;partnerId=3D4711" target=3D"_blank">https://crimson-letter.herokua=
pp.com/template?contractId=3D996315077&amp;partnerId=3D4711</a>
to display a web page for writing a letter.</p>
<p data-id=3D"f5e996f278c7373221b77af65c2d48de">They contain all the essent=
ial information necessary for the web page
to write the letter: <strong>the contract number and the partner number</st=
rong>.</p>
<p data-id=3D"0c71c945e86592d6572c682c871df29e">In
this way, the <em>letter</em> application can retrieve the data from the
backend simulator and render it in the web page. The coupling between the
main application and the letter application is very loose;
it=E2=80=99s just a link with two parameters. The main application does not
need to know what is behind the link. As a result, the <em>letter</em>
application can change its UI at any time without any impact on the
<em>portal</em> application.</p>
<p data-id=3D"65f081c7f9bd95f0264d829be13eb94f">However, all applications
use a common database from the backend simulator and are consequently
tightly coupled, because a change to the data would affect
the backend and the respective microservice</p>
